      "type": "event",
      "excerpt": "Born in Missouri to enslaved parents just two weeks before the Civil War ended, Carver was one of the most prominent black scientists of the 20th century.",
      "text": "Born in Missouri to enslaved parents just two weeks before the Civil War ended, Carver was one of the most prominent black scientists of the 20th century.\n\nLearning\n\nGeorge Washington Carver: Genius Behind The Peanut Man\n\nBorn in Missouri to enslaved parents just two weeks before the Civil War ended, Carver was one of the most prominent black scientists of the 20th century.\n\nAbout this event\n\nBorn in Missouri to enslaved parents just two weeks before the Civil War ended, Carver was one of the most prominent black scientists of the 20th century. He developed farming techniques to prevent soil depletion, encouraged poor farmers to grow peanuts and sweet potatoes to improve their quality of life, and was an early promoter of environmentalism. Registration for this Zoom program is required. Sign up here! This presentation is offered in collaboration with the Town of Pelham Public Library and other Westchester Libraries. Check the library page for registration, tickets, and any schedule updates.\n\nAt a Glance\n\nStatus\n\nPast\n\nDate\n\nThursday, June 18, 2026\n\nTime\n\n11:00 AM - 12:00 PM\n\nLocation\n\nWhite Plains Public Library\n\nAddress\n\n100 Martine Avenue, White Plains, NY 10601\n\nSource\n\nWhite Plains Public Library\n\nMore Events\n\nRelated events\n\nCANCELED The Birth of NY in White Plains (History via Igloo)\n\nJun 16, 2026\n\nEVENT CANCELED\n\nAm erica 250: The Importance of Revolutionary War Pensions\n\nJun 23, 2026\n\nIt's Am erica's 250th birthday, and for researchers across the country, Revolutionary War Pensions offer up valuable information about a veteran's life,…\n\nThe Birth of NY in White Plains (History via Igloo)\n\nJun 25, 2026\n\nLearn about why the 1776 approval of the Declaration of Independence, specifically at the White Plains court house, was such a big deal for New York and…"
