      "sourceId": "page:/events/library-revolutionary-sounds-2026-07-02/",
      "title": "REVOLUTIONARY SOUNDS!",
      "url": "/events/library-revolutionary-sounds-2026-07-02/",
      "type": "event",
      "excerpt": "Revolutionary Sounds explores the rich cultural exchange between European and African music in colonial New York.",
      "text": "Revolutionary Sounds explores the rich cultural exchange between European and African music in colonial New York.\n\nLearning\n\nREVOLUTIONARY SOUNDS!\n\nRevolutionary Sounds explores the rich cultural exchange between European and African music in colonial New York.\n\nAbout this event\n\nRevolutionary Sounds explores the rich cultural exchange between European and African music in colonial New York. Through live performance, storytelling, and hands-on demonstrations, students are introduced to historical instruments like the mountain dulcimer, djembe, banjo, and shekere. The program also highlights the significance of Pinkster, one of the earliest African Am erican holidays, and how music served as a vital thread connecting communities across cultures. With engaging songs, body percussion, and interactive call-and-response segments, Revolutionary Sounds brings history to life while illuminating the roots of Am erican music traditions like jazz and rock and roll. #RW250 Check the library page for registration, tickets, and any schedule updates.\n\nAt a Glance\n\nStatus\n\nPast\n\nDate\n\nThursday, July 2, 2026\n\nTime\n\n6:30 PM - 8:00 PM\n\nLocation\n\nWhite Plains Public Library\n\nAddress\n\n100 Martine Avenue, White Plains, NY 10601\n\nSource\n\nWhite Plains Public Library\n\nMore Events\n\nRelated events\n\nSummer Reading Kickoff featuring Jumpin' Jamie!\n\nJun 27, 2026\n\nCome start your summer vacation at the White Plains Public Library!\n\nCANCELED The Birth of NY in White Plains (History via Igloo)\n\nJun 16, 2026\n\nEVENT CANCELED\n\nThe Birth of NY in White Plains (History via Igloo)\n\nJun 25, 2026\n\nLearn about why the 1776 approval of the Declaration of Independence, specifically at the White Plains court house, was such a big deal for New York and…"
